-- Animal Hospital module for Serenity. Standalone shell, same pattern as UE/QOD/MM2 --
-- no shared runtime/theme table to hook into, so this builds its own small panel matching
-- the hub's look. First feature: ESP for patients and anomalies.
--
-- Classification (from reading the actual place file's client-replicated scripts, since
-- there's no live game access to test against): the workspace.NPCs folder is where every
-- active patient/anomaly/visitor model lives, watched directly for real-time add/remove.
-- A confirmed real patient carries GetAttribute("IsPatient") == true, a visitor
-- GetAttribute("IsVisitor") == true. Most anomaly templates under
-- ReplicatedStorage.NPCs.Anomalies get cloned in without a randomized name, so model.Name
-- itself usually identifies them; a handful also carry their own persistent tag, found the
-- same way: HasTag("GhostAnomaly") (Ghost), HasTag("AnomalyShadow") (Shadow),
-- HasTag("StalkerJumpscare") (Stalker), HasTag("TallMonsterSpawn") (Tall Monster),
-- GetAttribute("IsSlimebert") (Slimebert). "Unique" covers the wildcard lobby extras
-- (Barney, the Clown, Officer Duckman, etc. from ReplicatedStorage.NPCs.UniqueVisitors).
--
-- The patient-that-turns-out-to-attack-you case (Skinwalker/Mimic disguised as a normal
-- patient) traces back to ReplicatedStorage.AnomalyEvents.DisguiseReveals -- see the
-- longer comment right above ClassifyNPC below for how that one's actually detected, found
-- by reading the reveal-effect modules themselves plus a live NPC you flagged in testing.
-- An NPC that matches nothing at all still shows up as yellow "Unknown" rather than being
-- silently skipped or guessed at as a safe patient.
